(vector (map (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:actor/scope-excluded-ops (vector :finalize-installation-execution-decision :authorize-installation-execution :proceed-with-installation :authorize-network-electrical-compliance-clearance :finalize-network-electrical-compliance-clearance-decision :override-site-safety-officer-judgment :bypass-site-safety-officer-judgment)), (:actor/robotics? true), (:adr/related (vector "2799007412-cloud-itonami-isco-7412-electrical-mechanics-fitters-coverage")), (:adr/status :accepted), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "proceed with the installation" "finalize the installation-execution decision" "finalize the installation execution decision" "authorize the installation-execution" "authorize the installation execution" "authorize the network/electrical compliance clearance" "authorize the network electrical compliance clearance" "finalize the network/electrical compliance clearance decision" "finalize the network electrical compliance clearance decision" "override the site safety officer's judgment" "override the site safety officer judgment" "bypass the site safety officer's judgment" "bypass the site safety officer judgment")), (:adr/id 2799007422), (:phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:flag-safety-concern :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}}}"), (:actor/domain :trades/ict-installation-service-coordination), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-supply-order)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :installer-verified :site-verified :effect-propose :op-not-allowed :scope-exclusion)), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:actor/isco-08 "7422"), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-safety-concern)), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:adr/body "# ADR 2799007422: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7422 Information and Communications Technology Installers and Servicers site scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7422\n-- Information and Communications Technology Installers and\nServicers** only, Wave3 (production/trades) of the rollout, continuing\nthe batch that already landed 48 of the Wave3 occupations across\nbatches 1-8, including the closely-related 7412 (Electrical Mechanics\nand Fitters), 7413 (Electrical Line Installers and Repairers) and 7421\n(Electronics Mechanics and Servicers).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7422\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"7422\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Information and Communications\nTechnology Installers and Servicers\" -- confirming the premise, no\nmismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with\n`:required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no\n`:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7422` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the\n`7422` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nInformation and Communications Technology Installers and Servicers\ninstall and service network cabling and equipment on active job\nsites, in offices, data centers, rooftops and attics -- real\nelectrical-shock hazard (low-voltage but real) and working-at-height\nhazard (cable runs, rooftop/attic installs). The controlling\nconstraint for this vertical is that the closed op allowlist must\nNEVER include any op that directly finalizes an\ninstallation-execution decision or a network/electrical-compliance-\nclearance determination, nor override a site safety officer's\njudgment -- those decisions must always be a hard permanent block,\nnever auto-commit-eligible. This actor is deliberately scoped to SITE\nSCHEDULING/LOGISTICS COORDINATION ONLY -- it never performs\ninstallation work or makes compliance-clearance decisions itself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7412`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7412)\n(Electrical Mechanics and Fitters, same wave, closest domain shape:\n`store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`actor` split, `langgraph.graph`\nStateGraph, full doc set, certification-gated technician-safety\nclosed-allowlist + always-escalate safety flag + content-based\nscope-exclusion with a self-trip regression test) -- cloned and read\nin full in this session.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7422` as an OSS site\nscheduling/logistics-coordination actor for ICT installers and\nservicers, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every\nprior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **ICT Installation\nAdvisor ⊣ ICTInstallGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with\nan independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **site scheduling/logistics coordination\nONLY, never direct installation-execution or compliance-clearance\nauthority** -- it must NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate\nexercising, or propose exercising an installation-execution decision,\na network/electrical-compliance-clearance determination, or any\noverride of a site safety officer's judgment. These are not merely\ngated behind escalation -- they are structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- installation-job/progress data logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/site/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface an electrical-hazard/working-at-\n  height/equipment-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER\n  auto-commit eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- cabling/networking-equipment\n  procurement proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** an\ninstallation-execution decision or a network/electrical-compliance-\nclearance determination, and none that overrides a site safety\nofficer's judgment. These are **structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind escalation -- an op\nsuch as `:finalize-installation-execution-decision` cannot even be\nproposed by the reference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for`\nfunctions only ever emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any\ncode path (a forged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving\nfork) ever produced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD\ncheck (`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently. A\nsecond, independent HARD check (`:scope-excluded-action`) names seven\nconcretely-forbidden ops across both scope-exclusion categories\n(`ictinstall.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-installation-execution-decision`,\n`:authorize-installation-execution`, `:proceed-with-installation`,\n`:authorize-network-electrical-compliance-clearance`,\n`:finalize-network-electrical-compliance-clearance-decision`,\n`:override-site-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`:bypass-site-safety-officer-judgment`) as defense-in-depth, and is\nverified by seven dedicated unit tests\n(`ictinstall.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-installation-execution-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-installation-execution`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-installation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-network-electrical-compliance-clearance`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-network-electrical-compliance-clearance-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-site-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-bypass-site-safety-officer-judgment`).\n\n### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Installer unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered installer record, including certification\n   status (`:no-installer`). Never trusts the proposal's own claim of\n   who the installer is.\n2. **Site unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an independently\n   registered installation site (`:no-site`). Together with check 1,\n   this directly implements the CRITICAL invariant that an\n   installer/site record must be independently verified/registered\n   before any action.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)\n   (`:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),\n   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the seven\n   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`\n   names a finalization/execution action for proceeding with an\n   installation, authorizing or finalizing a network/electrical\n   compliance clearance, or overriding a site safety officer's\n   judgment, is a second HARD, PERMANENT block\n   (`:scope-excluded-action`), evaluated unconditionally on content --\n   defense-in-depth on top of the allowlist check: even if a future\n   change somehow widened the allowlist, content-based rejection still\n   independently blocks installation-execution/network-electrical-\n   compliance-clearance/site-safety-officer-override language wherever\n   it appears in free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface an electrical-shock-\n  risk/working-at-height/equipment-condition observation -- it never\n  resolves the concern itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is\n  the hazard data,\" never \"here is what I recommend doing about site\n  safety.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an\n  over-threshold cabling/networking-equipment order is not itself\n  unsafe, unlike an installation-execution or network/electrical-\n  compliance-clearance attempt, which is why it is handled by a\n  different mechanism (soft escalate vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"installation\", \"electrical\", \"network\", \"safety officer\") can\naccidentally match inside the mock advisor's own default rationale\ntext for a legitimate proposal, self-blocking the actor's own happy\npath -- this is exactly the risk this vertical's own vocabulary (a\nsite coordination actor working adjacent to live network cabling,\nelectrical circuits and certified site safety officers necessarily\ndiscusses those concepts to do its scheduling/logging job) could\ntrigger if phrased carelessly.\n`ictinstall.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased exclusively as\nfinalization/execution ACTIONS across both scope-exclusion categories\n(e.g. \"proceed with the installation\", \"authorize the\nnetwork/electrical compliance clearance\", \"override the site safety\nofficer's judgment\" -- matching the task's own worked example phrasing\nstyle), never bare nouns, and\n`ictinstall.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale\ntemplate was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (\"logged\nwork record for installer ...\", \"scheduled crew operation for network\ncabling installation task ...\", \"... routed for certified site safety\nofficer review\") WITHOUT containing any of the full finalization-\naction phrases, so the regression test is a genuine stress test of the\nbare-noun-vs-action-phrase distinction, not a vacuous pass. Green on\nfirst run in this session -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard\nitself was never tripped during development.\n`ictinstall.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`,\n`.../holds-a-network-electrical-compliance-clearance-op-even-at-high-confidence`\nand\n`.../holds-an-override-site-safety-officer-op-even-at-high-confidence`\nadditionally exercise the same invariant through the full graph run\nfor each of the two scope-exclusion categories, confirming a\nscope-excluded proposal never reaches `commit-record!` regardless of\ndisposition path.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7422\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7422` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7422`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 27 tests, 59 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-installation-execution-decision`\n  that is merely gated behind an always-escalate list (the shape some\n  earlier `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors use for approval-style ops):\n  rejected outright per the task's explicit domain-design guidance --\n  an always-escalate op still means the *capability* to finalize an\n  installation-execution decision exists in the code path and is\n  merely deferred to a human gate. For a decision this severe\n  (electrical shock, fall from height), the capability itself must not\n  exist. The only op related to safety escalation is\n  `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces the need for\n  human/certified-site-safety-officer attention and never itself\n  decides, authorizes, or defers an installation-execution decision\n  for later automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring some\n  reference actors' transaction-amount-ceiling HARD checks): rejected\n  per the task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold cabling/\n  networking-equipment order is not itself unsafe, so it is handled as\n  a soft escalate gate, not a hard block; only the\n  installation-execution/network-electrical-compliance-clearance/\n  site-safety-officer-override classes are a hard, unconditional\n  block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"installation\"\n  \"electrical\" \"network\" \"safety officer\"]`): rejected outright per\n  the fleet-known self-trip bug class described above (and CLAUDE.md's\n  own worked-example warning for this exact task, which explicitly\n  calls out phrasing scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution\n  ACTIONS never bare nouns), and per the explicit instruction to write\n  a dedicated test asserting default mock-advisor proposals never\n  self-trip -- this actor's own domain necessarily discusses\n  installation/electrical/network/safety-officer concepts to do its\n  scheduling/logging job, so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking\n  the actor's own legitimate proposals (regression-tested directly\n  against rationale text that deliberately contains those bare\n  nouns).\n- Collapsing the two scope-exclusion categories (installation-\n  execution, network/electrical-compliance-clearance) plus the site-\n  safety-officer-override into a single generic \"safety-override\"\n  op/category: rejected -- CLAUDE.md's CRITICAL section names these as\n  textually distinct finalization/execution actions, and collapsing\n  them would blur the defense-in-depth op-naming and phrase-matching\n  granularity that makes the self-trip regression test a genuine\n  stress test rather than a coincidence; each category also has a\n  semantically distinct real-world referent (executing an installation\n  vs. clearing a network/electrical compliance determination vs.\n  overriding a person's judgment) worth naming and testing\n  independently.\n- Merging `installer` and `site` into a single entity (only checking\n  one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape\n  (independently-registered installer AND independently-registered\n  site) matches the CRITICAL invariant that an \"installer/site record\n  must be independently verified/registered before any action\" and the\n  established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors with\n  two-sided provenance checks.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- installation-job/progress data logging\n   only. Requires an independently registered installer AND site\n   (`:no-installer`/`:no-site`). Not an installation-execution or\n   network/electrical-compliance-clearance decision in any respect --\n   it is a records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/site/task scheduling\n   proposal only. Cannot carry an installation-execution decision\n   (structurally absent from the op's field shape; any such attempt\n   via free text is caught by the `:scope-excluded-action` content\n   check). Does not authorize, finalize, or draw any conclusion about\n   a specific installation or network/electrical compliance clearance\n   -- it is a calendar/logistics action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces an electrical-shock-risk/\n   working-at-height/equipment-condition concern to a human/certified\n   site safety officer; ALWAYS escalates, unconditionally, at every\n   confidence level, NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY\n   channel through which a safety observation may be surfaced, and it\n   explicitly never resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself --\n   the certified site safety officer reviews and acts entirely on\n   their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- cabling/networking-equipment\n   procurement proposal only (item, cost). Not an installation-\n   execution, network/electrical-compliance-clearance, or\n   safety-officer-override decision of any kind -- it is a\n   purchasing-logistics action, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling\n   escalation (above 2000 always escalates).\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\nan installation-execution decision (e.g. proceeding with a specific\ncable/equipment installation), a network/electrical-compliance-\nclearance determination (e.g. authorizing the compliance clearance),\nor a site-safety-officer-judgment override -- these categories are\nstructurally absent, not gated, confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op\nallowlist itself (`ictinstall.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) seven\nconcretely-named forbidden-op fixtures rejected at the governor-unit\nlevel (`ictinstall.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`ictinstall.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-installation-execution-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-installation-execution`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-installation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-network-electrical-compliance-clearance`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-network-electrical-compliance-clearance-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-site-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-bypass-site-safety-officer-judgment`),\nand (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`ictinstall.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,\n`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against rationale\ntext that attempts to proceed with an installation, authorize a\nnetwork/electrical compliance clearance, or override a site safety\nofficer's judgment even via an otherwise-allowlisted op\n(`ictinstall.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-proceed-with-installation-even-with-allowlisted-op`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-authorize-network-electrical-compliance-clearance-even-with-allowlisted-op`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-site-safety-officer-judgment`).\n"), (:adr/date "2026-07-18"), (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 2000}"), (:test/counts "{:total 27, :assertions 59, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/name "Information and Communications Technology Installers and Servicers Coordination Practice"), (:db/id -1), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :installer-verification :site-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation)), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-7422"), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7422"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7422)), (:test/simulator :ictinstall.actor-test), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-7422 — Information and Communications Technology Installers and Servicers Site Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-7422)))